<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We evaluate our proposed WISER on three ZS-CIR benchmarks: <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fashion-IQ</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIRR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIRCO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Fashion-IQ focuses on fashion retrieval with three subcategories (Dress, Shirt, Toptee),
We use Recall@10, Recall@50 as the evaluation metric, and their mean across the three categories.
CIRR is the first natural image dataset designed specifically for CIR, built from real-life images in an open domain. We report Recall@k <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k\in\{1,5,10,50\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k\in\{1,5,10,50\})</annotation></semantics></math> and Recall<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{Subset}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Subset</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{Subset}}</annotation></semantics></math>@k (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>) following the benchmark protocol.
CIRCO is built from the COCO 2017 unlabeled set and is the first CIR dataset providing multiple ground truths per query. It contains 123,403 images in the database and 800 test queries. Following prior works, we report mAP@k (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{5,10,25,50\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{5,10,25,50\}</annotation></semantics></math>) as the evaluation metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
We adopt BAGEL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> as the editor, Qwen2.5-VL-7b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> as the verifier, and GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as the refiner.
Following previous methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, we use the pre-trained BLIP-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> as our captioner.
For the retrieval model, we experiment with different CLIP variants, including ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14, and ViT-G/14 CLIP from OpenCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
The retrieval candidate pool size <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 50, and the reliability threshold <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.7.
For uncertain cases, the refinement iteration is set to one round by default.
All experiments are implemented using PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> on a single NVIDIA H20 GPU, and the results are reported on official validation or test splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare WISER with a wide range of representative ZS-CIR baselines.
Specifically, PALAVRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, SEARLE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, LinCIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, MOA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and HIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> are textual inversion approaches originally designed or adapted for the ZS-CIR task.
We also include recent training-free methods such as CIReVL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, LDRE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, OSrCIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, AutoCIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and CoTMR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, which leverage LLMs or MLLMs without additional training. We also compare with IP-CIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> under the training-free setting reported in paper, which fuses T2I and I2I pathways using a fixed weight.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>ZS-CIR Benchmark Comparisons</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct comprehensive comparisons between WISER and state-of-the-art ZS-CIR methods on three benchmark datasets: CIRCO, CIRR, and Fashion-IQ. The results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ WISER: Wider Search, Deeper Thinking, and Adaptive Fusion for Training-Free Zero-Shot Composed Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ WISER: Wider Search, Deeper Thinking, and Adaptive Fusion for Training-Free Zero-Shot Composed Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIRCO.</span> As shown in the left section of Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ WISER: Wider Search, Deeper Thinking, and Adaptive Fusion for Training-Free Zero-Shot Composed Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, WISER demonstrates remarkable performance on the CIRCO dataset, which features multiple ground truths per query and clean annotations.
Based on these results, we have the following key observations:
(1) WISER substantially outperforms both training-free and training-based methods across all CLIP backbones. For instance, it achieves a relative improvement of 44.98% in mAP@5 over CoTMR with ViT-B/32, and surpasses LinCIR by 22.51% in mAP@5 with ViT-L/14, demonstrating the effectiveness of our framework.
(2) Furthermore, WISER consistently achieves superior mAP across all <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, thanks to the integration of candidates from both T2I and I2I pathways, which expands the search space and increases the likelihood of retrieving relevant targets under the multi-target nature of CIRCO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIRR.</span> The right section of Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ WISER: Wider Search, Deeper Thinking, and Adaptive Fusion for Training-Free Zero-Shot Composed Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents results on the challenging CIRR dataset, which is known for its high noise and weak correlation between reference images and target images.
The results lead to the following key observations:
(1) Despite the dataset’s inherent challenges, WISER delivers outstanding performance. With ViT-B/32, our method achieves a relative improvement of 56.98% in Recall@1 over the best baseline.
This underscores WISER’s robust capability in handling diverse and ambiguous modification intents.
(2) In the subset recall evaluation, which requires retrieving the correct image from six curated samples, WISER achieves 77.30% Recall<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>@1 with ViT-B/32, surpassing the second-best method by 10.69%. The improvement is more significant for smaller <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming its effectiveness in ranking the most relevant images at the top, which is critical in practical applications.
(3) While some baselines show performance saturation with larger backbones, WISER exhibits consistent gains, with ViT-G/14 achieving a top-tier Recall@1 of 49.54%, far exceeding other methods. This scalability further validates the generalizability and robustness of our framework’s design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fashion-IQ.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ WISER: Wider Search, Deeper Thinking, and Adaptive Fusion for Training-Free Zero-Shot Composed Image Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the results on the Fashion-IQ validation set, which focuses on fine-grained attribute modifications in the fashion domain.
Fashion-IQ features strong correlations between reference and target images, requiring the model to understand semantic attributes while preserving the original style structure.
Based on the results, we obtain the following observations:
(1) WISER outperforms both training-free and training-dependent baselines using ViT-B/32 and ViT-L/14 by leveraging complementary information from both T2I and I2I pathways, ensuring better balance between semantic accuracy and visual consistency.
(2) With the backbone of ViT-G/14, WISER achieves comparable or even superior performance compared with training methods such as LinCIR despite lacking their training advantage, which is crucial in specialized domains. This further confirms our work’s strong generalization and effectiveness.</p>
</div>
